{
  "global_card_id": "supply_and_demand_zones",
  "global_canonical": "Supply and Demand Zones",
  "type": "merged_concept",
  "school": "SMC",
  "aliases": [
    "Supply Area",
    "Marking Supply and Demand Zones",
    "Drawing Supply and Demand Zones"
  ],
  "definition": "Supply and demand zones are price areas where aggressive institutional buying or selling created sharp, impulsive moves that mark the origin of a price shift. Demand zones form at the base of bullish impulses and act as support; supply zones form at the top of bearish impulses and act as resistance. Drawn as forward-projected rectangles from the originating candle(s) or cluster (including wicks), they represent order-flow imbalances expected to produce price reactions or reversals upon retest.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "SMC-Indicator-Supplement (Marking Supply and Demand Zones)": "A method of identifying price zones where aggressive buying or selling initiated strong moves. Demand zones are marked from the base of a bullish impulse (often the last red candle or first green candle); supply zones from the top of a bearish impulse. The specific candle(s) selected depend on the formation, including wicks for rejection areas.",
    "SMC-Indicator-Supplement (Supply and Demand Zones)": "Price areas where aggressive buying or selling created sharp, impulsive moves, marking the origin of the move. They represent order-flow imbalances, with demand zones acting as support and supply zones as resistance, expected to cause price rejections upon revisit.",
    "SMC-Strong-Weak-Supplement": "Price zones where past institutional buying or selling created strong highs or lows, identified by aggressive impulsive moves away from consolidation or a break of structure. The zone is drawn from the candles preceding the impulsive move (typically the last candle or cluster) and projected forward to act as future support or resistance upon retest."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "Locate a sharp, impulsive move away from a price region, marked by consecutive large-bodied momentum candles; the Indicator-Supplement specifies at least three such candles in the move direction.",
    "Mark the zone at the move's origin: draw a horizontal rectangle from the high to low of the chosen candle(s) or cluster (including wicks), and project it forward in time.",
    "For demand zones, mark from the base of the bullish impulse — the first green candle, the last opposing (bearish) candle(s) before the upsurge, or a wick extreme; for supply zones, mark from the top of the bearish impulse — the first red candle, the last opposing (bullish) candle(s), or the wick top.",
    "Choose the candle based on formation: if the first candle yields too small a zone, use the last opposing candle for a more valid area; if a wick shows clear rejection, draw from the wick extreme where the reversal began.",
    "Strong-Weak-Supplement requires that the move qualify via a breakout from consolidation or a break of structure (BOS); zones drawn from swing highs/lows that did not cause a BOS are weak and invalid.",
    "Indicator-Supplement adds confluence checks: verify an inefficiency such as a Fair Value Gap (FVG) within the move, confirm sufficient push distance beyond the zone relative to prior swings, and validate a favorable risk-to-reward with logical stop placement.",
    "Assess the higher time frame trend bias: in a bullish HTF focus on demand zones at prior support; in a bearish HTF focus on supply zones at prior resistance or FVG areas (Indicator-Supplement emphasis).",
    "Adjust the zone for fake-outs: if price briefly breaks beyond and reverses, extend the zone to include the candles before that break; update zones based on the most recent supply/demand shift, incorporating prior reactions (Strong-Weak-Supplement emphasis).",
    "On retest, wait for confirmation before entry: Strong-Weak-Supplement uses candlestick patterns (e.g., hammer, engulfing) or a momentum candle; Indicator-Supplement switches to a lower time frame seeking reversal signals (e.g., RSI divergence, change of character)."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Marking zones from slow, sideways price action that lacks impulsive momentum or order-flow imbalance.",
    "Drawing zones too narrowly (e.g., using only one candle) without including the full aggressive activity area, or too rigidly without accounting for fake-outs and wicks.",
    "Choosing the wrong candle/starting point, resulting in a zone that is too small, invalid, or ignoring price's previous reaction to an earlier zone.",
    "Ignoring wick rejections that define the actual origin of the move.",
    "Drawing zones from swing highs/lows that have not caused a break of structure (weak highs/lows); or marking every sharp move without confirming an FVG, BOS, or sufficient push distance.",
    "Not updating zones when newer supply/demand shifts occur, leaving outdated zones.",
    "Entering immediately on touch without waiting for confirmation (candlestick pattern, momentum signal, or lower-time-frame reversal); or impatiently forcing entries before price actually reaches the zone.",
    "Assuming price will always fully retest or reverse exactly at the zone; it may react before, need a deeper retracement, or reverse early.",
    "Using zones without first establishing the higher time frame trend bias, risking counter-trend trades.",
    "Assuming every zone break is a valid Change of Character without considering nearby liquidity pools or unfilled FVGs that may invalidate the reversal.",
    "Not adjusting limit orders for spread or partial zone touches, leading to missed entries (Marking-Zones source)."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "Supply and demand zones act as high-probability reversal areas: buy at demand zones expecting bullish reversals, sell at supply zones expecting bearish reversals, with stops placed beyond the zone to allow for wicks or fake-outs. Entry styles differ by source: the Marking-Zones card favors resting buy/sell limit orders just inside or slightly above/below the zone to account for spread and partial touches; the Indicator-Supplement card waits for a rejection on revisit and confirms on a lower time frame (RSI divergence, CHoCH), aligning the zone with the HTF trend, where a decisive break and close beyond the zone signals a trend-reversing change of character; the Strong-Weak card waits for a confirming candlestick pattern or momentum candle on retest and avoids forced entries, setting take-profit above the break of structure for longs or below prior resistance for shorts.",
  "merge_notes": "Sources agree on the core concept but emphasize different validation and entry frameworks. The Indicator-Supplement (synthesis) card layers strong SMC confluence requirements (FVG, BOS, HTF bias, push distance) and lower-time-frame reversal confirmation. The Strong-Weak card emphasizes BOS/breakout qualification, fake-out handling, zone updating, and candlestick/momentum confirmation. The original Marking-Zones card is the most mechanical, using limit-order placement with spread allowance and a minimum three-candle momentum rule. These are complementary rather than contradictory views on confirming and entering at the same structures.",
